WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.100
SameSite is a cookie attribute that helps control when cookies are sent with cross-site requests.

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:13.560
It is an important browser feature for reducing some CSRF exposure, but it should be treated as defense in depth.

3
00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:23.900
SameSite can reduce when ambient cookies travel, while antiforgery validation still provides an application-level request-intent check for protected state-changing workflows.

4
00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:27.300
SameSite values reflect different browser behaviors.

5
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:35.240
Strict is the most restrictive for cross-site contexts and can be appropriate for some sensitive workflows, but it may affect user experience.

6
00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:44.060
Lax is commonly used because it balances ordinary navigation with reduced cross-site sending in many state-changing contexts.

7
00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:49.120
None allows cross-site sending but requires Secure in modern browser behavior.

8
00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:58.040
SameSite=None with Secure is common when cross-site cookie behavior is intentionally needed, such as some embedded or federated scenarios.

9
00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:00.300
That choice should be explicit.

10
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:12.240
A cookie that needs cross-site behavior may be legitimate, but the surrounding workflow still needs authentication, authorization, antiforgery review where relevant, CORS review where browser reads are involved, and logging.

11
00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:22.920
Login flows, external identity providers, embedded scenarios, payment flows, partner integrations, and cross-site application architectures can complicate cookie settings.

12
00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:26.860
A setting that protects one workflow may break another.

13
00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:33.680
Teams should test browser behavior across realistic environments rather than relying only on local development.

14
00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:37.480
SameSite should not be presented as a complete CSRF solution.

15
00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:46.700
It is valuable, but browser behavior, compatibility, application design, credential type, and state-changing endpoint behavior all matter.

16
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:53.760
Antiforgery validation remains the normal ASP.NET Core control for protected unsafe browser requests.

17
00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:55.640
Document cookie intent.

18
00:01:55.740 --> 00:02:09.020
Which cookies represent authentication, which support antiforgery, which support correlation, which are development-only, which require cross-site behavior, and which should be limited to same-site flows.

19
00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:15.760
That documentation makes future changes easier to review and prevents accidental weakening during troubleshooting.
